progress! `h_sqrt` compiled (I can see it in context). But line 75 has \"no goals\" — the `convert this using 1` succeeded too early and `intro n` has nothing to do. And line 48's unsolved goal is the main theorem goal that needs to use `h_sqrt`. The issue is that `h_sqrt` already has the right type but I need to convert `√(‖h n - x‖²)` to `‖h n - x‖`. Let me simplify the final step:"},{"seq":93,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:24.111118216Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":94,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:31.874452300Z","text":"Wait, `pow 0` gives `x^0 = 1` which is wrong. I need to go from `‖hₙ-x‖ → 0` knowing `‖hₙ-x‖² → 0`. Actually, `h_sq.pow 2` would give `‖hₙ-x‖⁴ → 0`, not helpful. Let me think differently.\n\nThe simplest approach: `‖hₙ-x‖² → 0` and `‖hₙ-x‖ ≥ 0`. In ℝ, `|f|² → 0` iff `f → 0`. And `‖hₙ-x‖` is already nonneg. So `‖hₙ-x‖ = |‖hₙ-x‖|` and `|‖hₙ-x‖|² = ‖hₙ-x‖²`. Use `tendsto_abs_atTop` and composition. Actually, the cleanest: since `f² → 0` and `f ≥ 0`, we have `f → 0` because `f = sqrt(f²)` and `sqrt` is continuous."},{"seq":95,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:31.874965323Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":96,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:38.359167082Z","text":"This is getting too complicated. Let me use a much simpler approach — `Tendsto.congr'` or just the definition of tendsto with `Real.sqrt_sq`:"},{"seq":97,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:38.359699606Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":98,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:45.348966926Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":99,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:51.675912033Z","text":"The parameter name is `f₂` not `g`. Let me fix:"},{"seq":100,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:51.676877840Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":101,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:51:59.503022649Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":102,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:52:07.920417530Z","text":"The `Tendsto.congr'` signature is `(f₁ =ᶠ[l] f₂) → Tendsto f₁ l₁ l₂ → Tendsto f₂ l₁ l₂`. So it takes an eventually-equality and a tendsto of `f₁`, giving tendsto of `f₂`. I have the tendsto of the RHS (sqrt) and want the LHS (norm). So the direction is reversed. Let me just provide the `f₁ = f₂` differently:"},{"seq":103,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:52:07.921000378Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":104,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:52:17.738165698Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":105,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:52:22.652087472Z","text":"Almost! The `congr` method takes `(∀ x, f₁ x = f₂ x)` where the tendsto is of `f₂` and the goal is of `f₁`. But my `this` is tendsto of `Real.sqrt (...)` and the goal is tendsto of `‖h n - x‖`. So I need `‖h n - x‖ = Real.sqrt(...)` which is `Real.sqrt_sq` directly (not `.symm`):"},{"seq":106,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:52:22.652459646Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":107,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:52:31.621216700Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -10"]},{"seq":108,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:52:51.093589938Z","text":"Part (a) compiles! Only the `sorry` in part (b) and an unused variable warning remain. Let me now tackle part (b). The key idea:\n\nFrom `0 ≤ ‖hₙ - x‖² = ‖hₙ‖² - 2⟨hₙ,x⟩ + ‖x‖²`:\n- `2⟨hₙ,x⟩ ≤ ‖hₙ‖² + ‖x‖²`\n- Taking liminf of both sides with the fact `⟨hₙ,x⟩ → ‖x‖²`:\n- `2‖x‖² ≤ liminf(‖hₙ‖²) + ‖x‖²`\n- So `‖x‖² ≤ liminf(‖hₙ‖²)`, hence `‖x‖ ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖`.\n\nThe tricky part is handling liminf properly. Let me use the key identity more directly. From `‖hₙ-x‖² ≥ 0`:\n- `‖x‖² = 2⟨hₙ,x⟩ - ‖hₙ‖² + ‖hₙ-x‖² ≥ 2⟨hₙ,x⟩ - ‖hₙ‖²`\n- Since `‖hₙ-x‖² ≥ 0`, we have `‖x‖² ≥ 2⟨hₙ,x⟩ - ‖hₙ‖²`\n- Actually that's wrong. From `0 ≤ ‖hₙ‖² - 2⟨hₙ,x⟩ + ‖x‖²`:\n- `2⟨hₙ,x⟩ - ‖hₙ‖² ≤ ‖x‖²`\n- Taking liminf: `liminf(2⟨hₙ,x⟩ - ‖hₙ‖²) ≤ ‖x‖²`\n- But `2⟨hₙ,x⟩ → 2‖x‖²`, so `liminf(2⟨hₙ,x⟩ - ‖hₙ‖²) = 2‖x‖² - limsup(‖hₙ‖²)`... this is messy.\n\nBetter approach: from `‖hₙ-x‖² ≥ 0`, rearrange:\n- `2⟨hₙ,x⟩ ≤ ‖hₙ‖² + ‖x‖²`\n- Since `⟨hₙ,x⟩ → ‖x‖²`, for any ε > 0, eventually `⟨hₙ,x⟩ > ‖x‖² - ε`\n- So eventually `2(‖x‖² - ε) < ‖hₙ‖² + ‖x‖²`, i.e., `‖hₙ‖² > ‖x‖² - 2ε`\n- This means `‖x‖² - 2ε ≤ ‖hₙ‖²` eventually, so `‖x‖² - 2ε ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖²`\n- Since ε is arbitrary, `‖x‖² ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖²`.\n\nFor the final step `‖x‖² ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖²` implies `‖x‖ ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖`:\nSince `t ↦ t²` is increasing on `[0,∞)` and `‖hₙ‖ ≥ 0`, if `a² ≤ liminf(bₙ²)` then... actually this isn't straightforward because liminf doesn't commute with sqrt in general. But we can use `Real.sqrt` monotonicity and continuity.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: prove `‖x‖² ≤ liminf(‖hₙ‖²)` and then note that for nonneg sequences, `liminf(aₙ²) = (liminf aₙ)²` when... no, that's not true either. Let me just prove `‖x‖ ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖` directly from the eventual inequality `‖hₙ‖ > ‖x‖ - 2ε` (taking square root isn't needed).\n\nWait, from `‖hₙ‖² > ‖x‖² - 2ε`, since both sides are nonneg (for small ε), `‖hₙ‖ > sqrt(‖x‖² - 2ε)`. For any δ < ‖x‖, pick ε small enough that `sqrt(‖x‖² - 2ε) > ‖x‖ - δ`. Then eventually `‖hₙ‖ > ‖x‖ - δ`, so `‖x‖ - δ ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖`. Since δ is arbitrary …[trimmed]"},{"seq":109,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:52:51.094059769Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":110,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:04.107148161Z","text":"Let me think more carefully about part (b). The `filter_upwards` approach is right but I need to handle the tendsto properly. The key inequality: for each `n`:\n`‖hₙ-x‖² ≥ 0` implies `2⟨hₙ,x⟩ ≤ ‖hₙ‖² + ‖x‖²`.\n\nSo if `⟨hₙ,x⟩ > ‖x‖² - ε`, then `‖hₙ‖² > 2(‖x‖² - ε) - ‖x‖² = ‖x‖² - 2ε`.\n\nSo for any `a < ‖x‖²`, pick `ε = (‖x‖² - a)/2 > 0`. Then eventually `⟨hₙ,x⟩ > ‖x‖² - ε`, so `‖hₙ‖² > ‖x‖² - 2ε = a`.\n\nI need `filter_upwards [h_inner]` to extract the eventual condition. Since `h_inner : Tendsto (fun n => ⟪h n, x⟫_ℝ) atTop (𝓝 (‖x‖^2))`, I need to convert this to \"eventually `⟨hₙ,x⟩ > ‖x‖² - ε`\". Use the nhds basis:"},{"seq":111,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:04.107683040Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":112,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:12.394076139Z","text":"Now for the second step: `‖x‖² ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖²` implies `‖x‖ ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖`. This follows from `liminf` monotonicity under `t ↦ sqrt(t)` for nonneg reals, but it's not direct. Let me use a similar approach: for any `a < ‖x‖`, eventually `a ≤ ‖hₙ‖`. From `‖hₙ‖² ≥ ‖x‖² - 2ε` eventually (from step 1), and `a² < ‖x‖²`, pick `ε` so that `‖x‖² - 2ε > a²`. Then eventually `‖hₙ‖² > a²`, so `‖hₙ‖ > |a| ≥ a`."},{"seq":113,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:12.394737876Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":114,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:26.442158771Z","text":"This is getting messy. Let me simplify step 2. The key fact: `le_liminf_of_le` requires `∀ᶠ n in atTop, a ≤ ‖h n‖`. I can derive this from step 1 which says `∀ᶠ n in atTop, a² ≤ ‖h n‖²` for any `a² < ‖x‖²`. But I need to connect these.\n\nActually, let me use a much cleaner approach. Since `h_sq_le` proves `‖x‖² ≤ liminf(‖hₙ‖²)`, and I know that `liminf(‖hₙ‖²) = (liminf ‖hₙ‖)²` when... no, that's not true in general. But I can prove `‖x‖ ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖` directly using the same technique:\n\nFor any `a < ‖x‖` with `a ≥ 0`: `a² < ‖x‖²`, so eventually `a² < ‖hₙ‖²`, hence `a < ‖hₙ‖` (since both nonneg). Use `sq_le_sq'`.\n\nLet me rewrite step 2 cleanly:"},{"seq":115,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:26.442817771Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":116,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:39.033527443Z","text":"This approach is getting too complicated with the liminf. Let me take a completely different strategy for part (b). Instead of going through `liminf` definition, let me directly use the same approach as in step 1 but for `‖x‖ ≤ liminf ‖hₙ‖` (not the squared version):\n\nFor any `a < ‖x‖` with `a ≥ 0`: pick `ε = (‖x‖ - a)/2 > 0`. Eventually `⟨hₙ,x⟩ > ‖x‖² - ε'` for appropriate `ε'`. From `‖hₙ-x‖² ≥ 0`: `‖hₙ‖² ≥ 2⟨hₙ,x⟩ - ‖x‖²`. Need `2⟨hₙ,x⟩ - ‖x‖² > a²`. So need `⟨hₙ,x⟩ > (a² + ‖x‖²)/2`. Since `⟨hₙ,x⟩ → ‖x‖²` and `(a² + ‖x‖²)/2 < ‖x‖²` (because `a < ‖x‖` implies `a² < ‖x‖²`), eventually `⟨hₙ,x⟩ > (a² + ‖x‖²)/2`.\n\nThis avoids the intermediate `liminf` entirely:"},{"seq":117,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:39.034333558Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":118,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:43.223291547Z","text":"Now I can also remove the `h_sq_le` step since it's not needed anymore. Let me also clean up the unused `h_sq_le`. Actually, let me first check if it compiles, then clean up:"},{"seq":119,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:46.721701061Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -30"]},{"seq":120,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:53.871324897Z","text":"Good progress! The `le_liminf_of_le` has an autoParam `IsCoboundedUnder` that needs to be resolved. The `intro a ha` fails because the first goal is actually the autoParam proof. Let me provide it explicitly. The issue is that `le_liminf_of_le` has:\n```\ntheorem le_liminf_of_le {f : Filter β} {u : β → α} {a}\n    (hf : f.IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) u := by isBoundedDefault)\n    (h : ∀ᶠ n in f, a ≤ u n) : a ≤ liminf u f\n```\n\nSo I need to provide the `hf` argument. For `fun n => (‖h n‖ : ℝ)` with `atTop`, this is bounded below by 0. Let me provide it:"},{"seq":121,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:53:53.872477993Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":122,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:54:00.008599191Z","text":"Also fix the same issue for `h_sq_le`:"},{"seq":123,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:54:00.009156829Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":124,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:54:09.130579556Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -30"]},{"seq":125,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:54:09.333513772Z","text":"[auto-continue 3/8]"},{"seq":126,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:54:13.830567157Z","text":"The `hf` argument is an autoParam, not a regular argument. Let me handle it differently — use `isBoundedDefault` or provide it via tactic:"},{"seq":127,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:54:13.831119021Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":128,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:54:17.883045Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":129,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:54:25.519742176Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd 