,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:55:29.384753560Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":146,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:55:37.885634934Z","text":"Actually, I realize I don't need `h_sq_le` at all for the direct proof in step 2. The direct proof already works from `le_liminf_of_le`. Let me just remove `h_sq_le` and handle the `IsCoboundedUnder` differently for step 2.\n\nLet me try using `Filter.IsCoboundedUnder` directly. For `atTop` on ℕ and `fun n => ‖h n‖ : ℝ` with `(· ≥ ·)`, I need to show the image is bounded below. Since `‖h n‖ ≥ 0`, it's bounded below by 0. Let me try:"},{"seq":147,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:55:38.056171244Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"IsCoboundedUnder.*atTop\\|isCobounded.*atTop\\|atTop.*IsCobounded\" .lake/packages/"]},{"seq":148,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:55:43.966817950Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"isCoboundedUnder_le_of_le\\|isCoboundedUnder_ge_of_le\\|IsCoboundedUnder.*ge\" .lak"]},{"seq":149,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:55:48.358043804Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Order/Filter/IsBounded.lean"]},{"seq":150,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:55:58.159702520Z","text":"`isCoboundedUnder_ge_of_le l hf` with `hf : ∀ i, f i ≤ x` gives `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) l f`. So for `fun n => ‖h n‖ : ℝ` with `∀ n, ‖h n‖ ≤ ?`, I need an upper bound, not lower. Wait — `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·)` means bounded below. And `hf : ∀ i, f i ≤ x` gives bounded above by x, which means... let me re-read.\n\nActually, `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u` means: `∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in f, a ≤ u x) → b ≥ a`. This says: if `a` is eventually a lower bound of `u`, then `a ≤ b`. So `b` is the supremum of eventual lower bounds. For this to hold, the sequence needs to be bounded below (so there exists at least one eventual lower bound). With `∀ i, u i ≥ x`, we get `x` is a lower bound, so this works.\n\nWait, the lemma says `hf : ∀ i, f i ≤ x` gives `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·)`. That's `f i ≤ x` (upper bound), not lower. This seems wrong for bounded below. Let me re-check... Actually I think `(· ≥ ·)` means the sequence is bounded *above* in the \"doesn't tend to infinity\" sense. For liminf, we need bounded below (cobounded under `≤`). \n\nLet me just try it directly — for `le_liminf_of_le` the `hf` defaults to `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u`, meaning bounded below. For `‖h n‖ ≥ 0`, use `isCoboundedUnder_ge_of_le` with an upper bound... no. Let me look at the actual lemma more carefully:"},{"seq":151,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:55:58.160903233Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Order/Filter/IsBounded.lean"]},{"seq":152,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:06.257000328Z","text":"OK so `isCoboundedUnder_le_of_le l hf` with `hf : ∀ i, x ≤ f i` gives `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≤ ·) l f` (bounded above, doesn't tend to +∞). And `isCoboundedUnder_ge_of_le l hf` with `hf : ∀ i, f i ≤ x` gives `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) l f` (bounded below, doesn't tend to -∞).\n\nWait, `le_liminf_of_le` needs `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u`. And `isCoboundedUnder_ge_of_le` provides exactly that when `∀ i, u i ≤ x` (bounded above). But I want bounded below by 0! \n\nActually, `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·)` means: the image doesn't tend to infinity in the `≥` direction. I.e., `∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in f, x ≥ a) → b ≥ a`. This says `b` is an upper bound on lower bounds. For a sequence bounded below by 0, every `a ≤ 0` is eventually ≤ all terms, so `a ≤ b` for any `b ≥ 0`. This holds with `b = 0`.\n\nSo `isCoboundedUnder_ge_of_le` with `∀ i, 0 ≤ ‖h n‖` doesn't directly apply (it needs `∀ i, f i ≤ x`). But I can use `isCoboundedUnder_le_of_le` with `∀ i, 0 ≤ ‖h n‖` to get `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≤ ·)`, which is the wrong relation.\n\nLet me just construct it manually:"},{"seq":153,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:06.258191052Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":154,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:09.645468267Z","text":"Now remove the unused `h_sq_le`:"},{"seq":155,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:09.646202955Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":156,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:13.466860544Z","text":"I see the `h_sq_le` is still there with a `sorry`. Let me remove it entirely since I'm proving part (b) directly:"},{"seq":157,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:13.467563851Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":158,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:30.011593013Z","text":"I see the issue — there's leftover code from `h_sq_le` that has a `sorry`, and then `intro a ha` at the wrong indentation level. Let me rewrite the entire part (b) cleanly:"},{"seq":159,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:30.011880567Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":160,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:30.011907988Z","text":"[edit_file ✗ old-string not found in /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean]"},{"seq":161,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:34.779711782Z","text":"The old_string doesn't match due to whitespace/content differences. Let me just rewrite the whole file:"},{"seq":162,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:34.780291455Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":163,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:56:51.607881337Z","tools":["write_file"],"previews":["write_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":164,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T1