08Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -30"]},{"seq":172,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:57:37.059709137Z","text":"The `rw [Filter.IsCobounded]` failed because it's a `def`, not unfoldable via rw. But `h_cob` itself was constructed (it's in the context). The issue is just that `rw` failed inside the `have`. The `apply le_liminf_of_le (hf := h_cob)` works since `h_cob` is now in scope. But line 97 shows `introN` fails — meaning the `apply` consumed both arguments at once. Let me check if the proof structure is right by looking at line 92-97:"},{"seq":173,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:57:37.060230719Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":174,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:57:42.521496186Z","text":"The `h_cob` proof has `rw` errors. Let me fix it differently — construct the `IsCoboundedUnder` without unfolding:"},{"seq":175,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:57:42.522110428Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":176,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:57:48.785709732Z","text":"Actually this is getting too complicated. Let me try yet another approach — provide the `IsCoboundedUnder` using `Filter.IsCoboundedUnder.of_frequently_ge` or a simpler tactic. Let me check what `iInf_le_liminf` actually does:\n\nThe key issue: `le_liminf_of_le` requires `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u`, which for `u = fun n => ‖h n‖` and `f = atTop` means the image is cobounded in the ≥ direction. Since `‖h n‖ ≥ 0` for all n, this should be trivially true.\n\nLet me try to construct it more directly:"},{"seq":177,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:57:48.786520026Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":178,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:57:56.027742666Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":179,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:05.438005356Z","text":"Wait — `h_cob` is now in context! The `intro a ha` at line 91 works (the `introN` error is from a different spot). The only error is `le_of_le_of_lt` not existing — it's `lt_of_le_of_lt` or I should use `le_trans`. But actually the proof of `h_cob` has an error. Let me look at what `IsCoboundedUnder` unfolds to. The definition is:\n`IsCoboundedUnder r f u = (map u f).IsCobounded r`\nAnd `IsCobounded r f = ∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in f, r x a) → r b a`.\n\nSo for `r = (· ≥ ·)`, `f = atTop`, `u = fun n => ‖h n‖`:\n`∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ y in map (fun n => ‖h n‖) atTop, y ≥ a) → b ≥ a`\n\nWith `b = 0`: need `∀ a, (∀ᶠ y in map ..., y ≥ a) → 0 ≥ a`.\n\nIf eventually all `‖h n‖ ≥ a`, and some `‖h n‖ ≥ 0` always, then... we need `0 ≥ a`. But that's only true if `a ≤ 0`. What if `a > 0`? If eventually `‖h n‖ ≥ a > 0`, then we need `0 ≥ a`, which is false!\n\nSo `0` doesn't work as the cobounder for all `a`. The `IsCobounded` definition says: `b` is such that any `a` that is eventually a lower bound satisfies `a ≤ b`. But `b` here is supposed to bound the eventual lower bounds from above. Since `‖h n‖` can be arbitrarily large, any `a` could be an eventual lower bound, and we'd need `b ≥ a`.\n\nActually, `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u` just means the image doesn't \"tend to infinity\" — i.e., it's bounded. Not bounded below, but bounded (in the sense that the liminf exists). For ℝ with `(· ≥ ·)`, it means: the filter `map u f` is cobounded under ≥, meaning the liminf is not -∞.\n\nWait, I think I'm confusing myself. Let me just look at how `iInf_le_liminf` handles it:"},{"seq":180,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:05.440247349Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Order/LiminfLimsup.lean"]},{"seq":181,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:10.141270032Z","text":"So `by isBoundedDefault` works for `iInf_le_liminf`. The key difference is that `iInf_le_liminf` is universally quantified, so Lean can infer the bound from the `ConditionallyCompleteLattice` structure. Let me just try `by isBoundedDefault` again — maybe the error was from the `h_cob` construction, not the tactic itself:"},{"seq":182,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:10.141917776Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":183,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:17.147361622Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":184,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:24.120632783Z","text":"`isBoundedDefault` still fails — it can't find a default bound for an arbitrary function `fun n => ‖h n‖`. The `iInf_le_liminf` case works because `⨅ n, u n` exists (provides the bound). \n\nLet me bypass `le_liminf_of_le` entirely and use `liminf_eq` directly:"},{"seq":185,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:24.121295630Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":186,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:47.291003509Z","text":"Now the goal is `a ≤ ‖x‖` given `ha : ∀ᶠ n in atTop, a ≤ ‖h n‖`. This means eventually `‖h n‖ ≥ a`. Combined with the norm identity and weak convergence, I can show `a ≤ ‖x‖`.\n\nFrom `ha`: eventually `‖h n‖ ≥ a`, i.e., `‖h n‖² ≥ a²` (if `a ≥ 0`).\nFrom `‖hₙ-x‖² ≥ 0`: `‖h n‖² ≥ 2⟨h n,x⟩ - ‖x‖²`.\nSo eventually `2⟨h n,x⟩ - ‖x‖² ≤ ‖h n‖²` and `a ≤ ‖h n‖`.\nAlso `⟨h n,x⟩ → ‖x‖²`, so eventually `⟨h n,x⟩ > ‖x‖² - ε`.\nThis gives `‖h n‖² ≥ 2(‖x‖² - ε) - ‖x‖² = ‖x‖² - 2ε`.\nCombined with `a ≤ ‖h n‖`: `a² ≤ ‖h n‖²` (if `a ≥ 0`).\nSo `a² ≤ ‖h n‖²` and `‖h n‖² ≥ ‖x‖² - 2ε` eventually.\nIf `a² > ‖x‖²`, then `‖x‖² - 2ε < a²` for small ε, contradiction since `a² ≤ ‖h n‖² ≥ ‖x‖² - 2ε`.\n\nActually, this direction (showing `a ≤ ‖x‖`) is a contradiction argument: assume `a > ‖x‖`, derive that `‖hₙ‖² < a²` eventually (from norm identity + weak convergence), contradicting `‖hₙ‖ ≥ a`.\n\nLet me write this:"},{"seq":187,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:47.291452483Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":188,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:58:47.291505548Z","text":"[edit_file ✗ old-string not found in /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean]"},{"seq":189,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:59:28.608811104Z","text":"I realized my approach with `le_csSup` is backwards. The `csSup` requires showing `‖x‖` is an upper bound of `{a | eventually a ≤ ‖h n‖}`. But I actually need to show `‖x‖ ≤ csSup(...)`, which means `‖x‖` is a lower bound... no. Let me think again.\n\n`liminf = csSup {a | eventually a ≤ u n}`. I want to show `‖x‖ ≤ liminf`. This means `‖x‖` is ≤ the supremum of the set `{a | eventually a ≤ ‖h n‖}`. This is equivalent to: for any `b < ‖x‖`, `b` is in the set, i.e., eventually `b ≤ ‖h n‖`.\n\nSo I should use `le_csSup` the other way: show the set contains elements arbitrarily close to `‖x‖`. The correct approach: for any `b < ‖x‖`, eventually `b ≤ ‖h n‖`. This is exactly what my earlier \"direct\" proof did.\n\nLet me rewrite the whole theorem (b) cleanly using this insight:"},{"seq":190,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:59:28.609436517Z","tools":["write_file"],"previews":["write_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":191,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:59:35.952540203Z","text":"I've been going around in circles with the liminf. Let me take a completely clean approach. The forward direction works: for any `a < ‖x‖`, eventually `a ≤ ‖h n‖`. This directly proves `‖x‖ ≤ liminf ‖h n‖` by the definition of liminf (as the supremum of eventual lower bounds). I need to show `‖x‖` is ≤ the supremum, which means every `c < ‖x‖` is in the set of eventual lower bounds.\n\nLet me use `isLUB` or the `le_csSup` correctly. `csSup S ≥ ‖x‖` iff every `c < ‖x‖` satisfies `c ∈ S` (when S is nonempty). This is `csSup_le_iff` or `le_csSup_iff`."},{"seq":192,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:59:35.959629448Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"csSup_le_iff\\|le_csSup_iff\\|lt_csSup_iff\\|csSup_lt_iff\" .lake/packages/mathlib/M"]},{"seq":193,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:59:39.638797555Z","text":"`lt_csSup_iff (hb : BddAbove s) (hs : s.Nonempty) : a < sSup s ↔ ∃ b ∈ s, a < b`. But this is for BddAbove. For liminf, it's `csSup` of a BddBelow set. Let me check `le_csSup_iff`:"},{"seq":194,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:59:39.640161995Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Order/ConditionallyCompleteLattice/Basic.lean"]},{"seq":195,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:02.099366688Z","text":"I see: `le_csSup_iff` gives `a ≤ sSup s ↔ ∀ b, b ∈ upperBounds s → a ≤ b`. And `csSup_le_iff` gives `sSup s ≤ a ↔ ∀ b ∈ s, b ≤ a`.\n\nFor liminf = `sSup {a | eventually a ≤ u n}`, I want `‖x‖ ≤ sSup S`. Using `le_csSup_iff`:\n`‖x‖ ≤ sSup S ↔ ∀ b, b ∈ upperBounds S → ‖x‖ ≤ b`\n\nThis means every upper bound of the set of eventual lower bounds is ≥ ‖x‖. Equivalently, there's no upper bound < ‖x‖.\n\nThis is getting very meta. Let me just use the forward direction with `le_csSup` correctly. The simplest: show `‖x‖ - ε ∈ S` for all `ε > 0`, which means `‖x‖ - ε ≤ csSup S` for all `ε > 0`, hence `‖x‖ ≤ csSup S`.\n\nBut `le_csSup : BddBelow S → a ∈ S → a ≤ csSup S`. So for each `ε > 0`, `(‖x‖ - ε) ∈ S` gives `‖x‖ - ε ≤ csSup S`. Then `‖x‖ ≤ csSup S` by the fact that `‖x‖ - ε → ‖x‖`.\n\nBut this requires handling the real analysis of limits, which is annoying. Let me just use `csSup_le` on an upper bound. Actually the simplest approach: just prove `∀ ε > 0, ‖x‖ - ε ≤ liminf` using `le_csSup` for each `‖x‖ - ε ∈ S`."},{"seq":196,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:02.100007265Z","tools":["write_file"],"previews":["write_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":197,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:10.754065335Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -30"]},{"seq":198,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:15.160127380Z","text":"The lemma `le_of_forall_lt_iff_le` doesn't exist. Let me check for the correct name:"},{"seq":199,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:15.233504975Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"forall_lt.*le\\|le_iff_forall_lt\\|le_of_forall_sub_lt\\|forall_lt_iff\" .lake/packa"]},{"seq":200,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:29.928803462Z","text":"There's `le_of_forall_lt_rat_imp_le` but that's for rationals. For reals, the standard approach is different. Let me use a simpler tactic — just handle it via `by_contra`:\n\nInstead of the `le_of_forall_lt_iff_le`, let me prove `‖x‖ ≤ liminf` by showing that for every `ε > 0`, `‖x‖ - ε ≤ liminf` via `le_csSup`. Or even simpler: just apply the definition directly using `liminf_eq` and `le_csSup` in the forward direction (showing `‖x‖ - ε` is in the set for any `ε > 0`).\n\nActually, the cleanest: just prove `∀ᶠ n in atTop, a ≤ ‖h n‖` for each `a < ‖x‖`, then use `le_liminf_of_le` but with the right coboundedness argument. Let me try providing the coboundedness by unfolding the definition manually:"},{"seq":201,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:29.929204867Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":202,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:37.709178869Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -30"]},{"seq":203,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:42.034755432Z","text":"Several errors. Let me fix them:\n1. `sq_lt_sq'` takes 3 args: `sq_lt_sq' h1 h2 h3` where `h1 : a < b`, `h2 : 0 ≤ a`, `h3 : 0 ≤ b`. I had it wrong.\n2. `Ioo_mem_nhds` gives `Ioo lo hi ∈ 𝓝 x` but I need `∀ᶠ y in 𝓝 x, lo < y`. Use `Ioo_mem_nhds` correctly or `eventually_nhds_within`.\n\nLet me check `sq_lt_sq'`:"},{"seq":204,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:42.040885428Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B1 -A2 \"theorem sq_lt_sq'\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Algebra/Order/Ring/ 2>/dev"]},{"seq":205,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:47.017481336Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"theorem sq_lt_sq'\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/ 2>/dev/null | head -3"]},{"seq":206,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:47.198450248Z","text":"[auto-continue 5/8]"},{"seq":207,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:51.007937016Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"sq_lt_sq\\|sq_le_sq\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Algebra/Order/Ring/Lemmas.lea"]},{"seq":208,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:56.845383976Z","text":"`sq_lt_sq' (h1 : -b < a) (h2 : a < b) : a^2 < b^2`. So it takes `-b < a` and `a < b`. For `a^2 < ‖x‖^2` with `0 ≤ a` and `a < ‖x‖`: `h1 = -‖x‖ < a` (true since `-‖x‖ ≤ 0 ≤ a`) and `h2 = a < ‖x‖`. Let me fix:"},{"seq":209,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:00:56.846182382Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":210,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:00.377845557Z","text":"Also fix the `sq_le_sq'` usage later:"},{"seq":211,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:00.378416699Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":212,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:06.059836185Z","text":"Wait, `sq_le_sq` gives `a^2 ≤ b^2 ↔ |a| ≤ |b|`. I need `a ≤ ‖h n‖` from `a^2 ≤ ‖h n‖^2` with `0 ≤ a` and `0 ≤ ‖h n‖`. Let me use `sq_le_sq₀`:"},{"seq":213,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:06.064171303Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B1 -A3 \"lemma sq_le_sq₀\\|theorem sq_le_sq₀\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Algebra/O"]},{"seq":214,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:09.737154270Z","text":"`sq_le_sq₀ (ha : 0 ≤ a) (hb : 0 ≤ b) : a^2 ≤ b^2 ↔ a ≤ b`."},{"seq":215,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:09.737724461Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":216,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:16.934324074Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -30"]},{"seq":217,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:22.792844919Z","text":"The `Ioc_mem_nhds` returns a set membership, not an `Eventually`. I need to convert. For `Tendsto.eventually`, I need `s ∈ 𝓝 (‖x‖^2)` and it gives `∀ᶠ n in atTop, ⟪h n, x⟫ ∈ s`. So I need to express my condition as a set membership. The issue is `Ioc_mem_nhds` gives `Ioc a b ∈ 𝓝 c` but I need the condition `(a^2 + ‖x‖^2)/2 < y` to be expressed as `y ∈ Ioc (a^2+‖x‖^2)/2 ∞` or just `y ∈ Ioi ((a^2+‖x‖^2)/2)`.\n\nLet me use `Ioi_mem_nhds`:"},{"seq":218,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:22.793549937Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":219,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:35.859844944Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":220,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:43.543756564Z","text":"One error at line 94. The `le_trans` in the `h_bdd` proof is wrong — `ha` is a membership proof, not a ≤. Let me fix:"},{"seq":221,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:43.544628983Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":222,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:01:53.281927080Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":223,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:02:15.144614135Z","text":"Multiple issues. The `lt_csSup_iff` needs `BddAbove` not `BddBelow`, and the `BddBelow` proof is wrong. Actually, for `csSup` on ℝ, the set needs to be `BddAbove` for `csSup` to make sense (it's the supremum). The set `{a | eventually a ≤ ‖h n‖}` is bounded above by... well, it's not necessarily bounded above. But liminf exists in extended reals.\n\nI'm spending too much time on this liminf machinery. Let me take the simplest possible approach: just commit a partial with part (a) complete and part (b) sorried, since the PACING instructions say to commit a partial if the full proof isn't in reach."},{"seq":224,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:02:15.145009653Z","tools":["write_file"],"previews":["write_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":225,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:02:26.590782116Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":226,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:02:33.749980185Z","text":"Issues: `le_csSup` needs `BddAbove` not `BddBelow` (for csSup = supremum), and the helper theorem is wrong. Let me fix:\n\nFor `csSup S`, the set S = `{a | eventually a ≤ ‖h n‖}` needs `BddAbove` for `csSup` to be finite. The set is bounded above because... well, it's not necessarily bounded above! `‖h n‖` could be unbounded. But `liminf` exists in the extended reals.\n\nActually, `csSup` in a `ConditionallyCompleteLattice` requires the set to be bounded above (BddAbove). For liminf, Mathlib uses `csSup {a | eventually a ≤ u n}` which requires BddBelow (since it's the sup of eventual lower bounds, and the lower bounds are bounded below by the infimum). Wait — `csSup` of a `BddBelow` set gives the sup. But `le_csSup` needs `BddAbove`. \n\nI think the issue is that `csSup` on ℝ requires `BddAbove`, not `BddBelow`. Let me check:\n\n`le_csSup : BddBelow s → a ∈ s → a ≤ csSup s` — actually in the `ConditionallyCompleteLattice`, `csSup` is defined for sets that are `BddAbove`. And `le_csSup` takes `BddBelow s` (so that `csSup` is not `-∞`). Let me just check:"},{"seq":227,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:02:33.754882939Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B1 -A2 \"theorem le_csSup\\b\\|lemma le_csSup\\b\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Order/C"]},{"seq":228,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:02:49.559671320Z","text":"`le_csSup (h₁ : BddAbove s) (h₂ : a ∈ s) : a ≤ sSup s`. So I need `BddAbove` for the set `{a | eventually a ≤ ‖h n‖}`. This set is bounded above iff the sequence `‖h n‖` is bounded above. For an arbitrary sequence in a Hilbert space, this may not be true!\n\nSo the `liminf_eq` uses `csSup` which may return `-∞` if the set is empty or unbounded. For the purpose of this proof, I should avoid `liminf_eq` and use a different formulation.\n\nLet me go back to `le_liminf_of_le` and provide the coboundedness correctly. Actually, I now realize: the `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·)` for liminf corresponds to the set being bounded below (not above). Since `‖h n‖ ≥ 0`, the set `{a | eventually a ≤ ‖h n‖}` contains 0 and is bounded below by 0 (any `a < 0` has `eventually a ≤ 0 ≤ ‖h n‖`, so `a` is in the set). So `BddAbove` is NOT the right condition; I need the set to be nonempty (which it is, since 0 is in it) and... actually `csSup` just needs the set to be `BddAbove`. The set `{a | eventually a ≤ ‖h n‖}` is bounded above iff there's some `M` such that no `a > M` is an eventual lower bound, which means the sequence isn't eventually above every bound — i.e., the sequence is bounded.\n\nThis is getting t